{"id":"how-the-internet-works","title":"How the internet works, for security","subject":"Cybersecurity","label":"Cybersecurity","exams":[],"language":"en","seconds":186,"duration":185.99,"audio":"voice.mp3","words":487,"sections":[{"id":"hook","title":"The puzzle","start":0,"end":12.725},{"id":"explain","title":"Core idea","start":12.725,"end":51.115},{"id":"explain2","title":"HTTP and HTTPS","start":51.115,"end":87.775},{"id":"example","title":"Worked example","start":87.775,"end":114.545},{"id":"check","title":"Quick check","start":114.545,"end":123.535},{"id":"answer","title":"The answer","start":123.535,"end":132.945},{"id":"mistakes","title":"Common mistakes","start":132.945,"end":154.615},{"id":"recap","title":"Recap and tip","start":154.615,"end":185.99}],"lines":[{"s":0,"a":0.5,"b":7.14,"text":"Have you ever typed a website address and watched the page appear, then wondered who carried it there? I'm Kora.","w":[0.52,0.66,0.82,1.04,1.36,1.44,1.86,2.62,2.78,3.1,3.2,3.5,4.36,4.6,4.94,5.12,5.44,5.52,6.5,6.72]},{"s":0,"a":7.44,"b":12.3,"text":"In the next three minutes, you'll see what happens on that journey, and why it matters for security.","w":[7.46,7.56,7.68,7.9,8.12,8.66,8.94,9.1,9.28,9.64,9.74,9.9,10.76,10.9,11.12,11.22,11.56,11.72]},{"s":1,"a":13.15,"b":17.99,"text":"The internet is a network of networks. Your data is split into small packets.","w":[13.18,13.4,13.88,14.02,14.14,14.52,14.62,15.68,15.9,16.3,16.46,16.86,17.12,17.42]},{"s":1,"a":18.29,"b":22.92,"text":"Each packet carries the destination address, so routers know where to send it next.","w":[18.36,18.6,19.04,19.4,19.52,20.14,21.02,21.24,21.68,21.88,22.06,22.16,22.4,22.5]},{"s":1,"a":23.22,"b":27.51,"text":"Routers pass those packets from network to network until they reach the right place.","w":[23.28,23.76,24.08,24.32,24.74,24.96,25.34,25.46,26.12,26.4,26.56,26.78,26.88,27.08]},{"s":1,"a":27.81,"b":32.99,"text":"Every device on a network has an IP address. Think of it as a postal address for your device.","w":[27.9,28.14,28.54,28.66,28.74,29.16,29.34,29.54,29.88,30.88,31.08,31.18,31.28,31.38,31.48,31.9,32.24,32.36,32.52]},{"s":1,"a":33.29,"b":40.58,"text":"DNS is the phone book of the internet. It turns a name you type, like a website name, into an IP address.","w":[33.32,34.2,34.32,34.48,34.76,34.96,35.06,35.18,36.18,36.3,36.6,36.7,36.92,37.1,37.74,37.94,38.04,38.56,39.18,39.44,39.7,40.04]},{"s":1,"a":40.88,"b":44.57,"text":"A port is a numbered door on a computer for one kind of service.","w":[40.94,41.04,41.42,41.56,41.7,42.1,42.48,42.58,42.66,43.14,43.46,43.64,43.88,44]},{"s":1,"a":44.87,"b":50.69,"text":"Web pages use port eighty for HTTP and port four hundred and forty three for HTTPS.","w":[44.9,45.12,45.56,45.74,46.06,46.32,46.64,47.68,47.84,48.16,48.4,48.7,48.86,49.16,49.46,49.8]},{"s":2,"a":51.54,"b":55.85,"text":"HTTP sends data as plain text. Anyone on the path could read it.","w":[51.7,52.54,52.82,53.16,53.3,53.58,54.5,54.86,54.94,55.06,55.34,55.52,55.74]},{"s":2,"a":56.15,"b":63.09,"text":"HTTPS encrypts that data with TLS. It also proves the site's identity with a certificate, shown by the padlock.","w":[56.34,57.42,57.86,58.06,58.34,58.56,59.7,59.82,60.12,60.42,60.56,60.86,61.3,61.46,61.54,62.22,62.48,62.6,62.72]},{"s":2,"a":63.39,"b":68.57,"text":"Remember, the padlock means the connection is private. It does not mean the site is honest.","w":[63.44,64.08,64.22,64.8,65.1,65.22,65.7,65.82,66.72,66.82,67.02,67.22,67.44,67.6,67.94,68.06]},{"s":2,"a":68.87,"b":74.69,"text":"Attackers may scan for open ports or read plain HTTP. Touching systems without permission is illegal.","w":[68.92,69.4,69.6,69.96,70.14,70.42,70.84,71,71.26,71.72,72.36,72.72,73.38,73.7,74.16,74.28]},{"s":2,"a":74.99,"b":78.76,"text":"A firewall is a filter that decides what traffic may come in or go out.","w":[75.06,75.18,75.7,75.82,75.92,76.3,76.46,76.94,77.14,77.56,77.74,77.98,78.3,78.42,78.64]},{"s":2,"a":79.06,"b":87.35,"text":"Defenders care about this. They close unused ports, use firewalls to filter traffic, and push for HTTPS everywhere.","w":[79.1,79.66,79.92,80.14,80.9,81.1,81.58,82.04,82.9,83.14,83.78,83.9,84.22,85.04,85.18,85.44,85.8,86.92]},{"s":3,"a":88.2,"b":92.48,"text":"Now follow a simple example. You type a website address and press enter.","w":[88.26,88.54,88.88,89,89.34,90.26,90.42,90.7,90.78,91.3,91.7,91.86,92.16]},{"s":3,"a":92.78,"b":96.68,"text":"First, your device asks DNS for the IP address behind that name.","w":[92.86,93.22,93.4,93.92,94.28,94.82,94.96,95.22,95.56,95.9,96.22,96.42]},{"s":3,"a":96.98,"b":100.75,"text":"Then it connects to the web server on port four hundred and forty three.","w":[97.02,97.22,97.32,97.68,97.8,97.92,98.22,98.74,98.9,99.32,99.58,99.88,100.04,100.4]},{"s":3,"a":101.05,"b":104.73,"text":"Your browser sends an encrypted request through HTTPS.","w":[101.06,101.26,101.7,102,102.1,102.64,103.22,103.66]},{"s":3,"a":105.03,"b":108.94,"text":"The server sends the page back, still encrypted, and your browser shows it.","w":[105.06,105.2,105.58,105.82,105.94,106.22,106.9,107.18,107.92,108.02,108.16,108.56,108.86]},{"s":3,"a":109.24,"b":114.12,"text":"If the site only offered HTTP, everything would travel as plain text instead.","w":[109.28,109.38,109.52,109.88,110.2,110.78,112.06,112.44,112.6,112.98,113.12,113.4,113.72]},{"s":4,"a":114.97,"b":120.47,"text":"Quick check. Which port does HTTPS use, and what does the padlock actually promise?","w":[115,115.28,116,116.26,116.52,116.9,117.86,118.3,118.44,118.62,118.78,118.9,119.54,119.94]},{"s":4,"a":120.92,"b":123.11,"text":"Pause here and try it. I'll wait.","w":[120.96,121.3,121.66,121.8,122.08,122.56,122.76]},{"s":5,"a":123.96,"b":132.52,"text":"HTTPS uses port four hundred and forty three. The padlock promises a private encrypted connection, not that the site is honest.","w":[124.14,125.24,125.54,125.94,126.2,126.54,126.7,127.02,127.82,127.96,128.48,129.06,129.16,129.58,130.06,131.14,131.32,131.46,131.6,131.86,132.02]},{"s":6,"a":133.37,"b":139.86,"text":"One common mistake is trusting the padlock as a sign of honesty. Check the site's purpose and reputation instead.","w":[133.54,133.78,134.14,134.62,134.8,135.24,135.36,135.94,136.06,136.16,136.46,136.58,137.56,137.82,137.96,138.28,138.7,138.84,139.46]},{"s":6,"a":140.16,"b":146.18,"text":"Another is leaving unused ports open. Close what you do not need, and let a firewall filter the rest.","w":[140.22,140.64,140.78,141.2,141.58,141.96,142.86,143.18,143.34,143.48,143.62,143.84,144.54,144.66,144.82,144.9,145.38,145.72,145.84]},{"s":6,"a":146.48,"b":154.19,"text":"A third is using HTTP for anything private. Use HTTPS everywhere, especially for logins and forms.","w":[146.58,146.74,147.16,147.32,147.86,148.78,149,149.36,150.46,150.88,151.86,152.56,153.06,153.22,153.68,153.8]},{"s":7,"a":155.04,"b":161.43,"text":"Recap. The internet moves packets through networks, using IP addresses and DNS to find the way.","w":[155.12,156.32,156.48,156.88,157.18,157.58,157.82,158.48,158.86,159.2,159.9,160.08,160.68,160.8,161.04,161.16]},{"s":7,"a":161.73,"b":167.87,"text":"Ports are numbered doors, and HTTPS protects web traffic on port four hundred and forty three.","w":[161.74,162.08,162.24,162.54,163.46,163.84,164.88,165.36,165.64,166.16,166.32,166.64,166.88,167.14,167.28,167.56]},{"s":7,"a":168.17,"b":174.5,"text":"Defenders close unused ports, filter traffic with firewalls, and choose HTTPS.","w":[168.2,168.84,169.34,169.78,170.74,171.12,171.52,171.68,172.74,172.88,173.4]},{"s":7,"a":174.8,"b":181.76,"text":"Top tip: before you enter personal data, check for HTTPS, then check whether the site deserves your trust.","w":[174.84,175.16,175.76,176.12,176.32,176.58,177,177.8,178.06,178.4,179.56,179.82,180.08,180.36,180.48,180.74,181.1,181.28]},{"s":7,"a":182.06,"b":185.39,"text":"Keep learning with Knowzone, where hard ideas become simple.","w":[182.08,182.34,182.64,182.82,183.66,183.92,184.22,184.64,185.02]}],"scenes":[{"at":0.25,"heading":"The puzzle","items":[{"at":0.4,"kind":"text","text":"Type address → page appears; who carries it?"},{"at":7.34,"kind":"text","text":"See the journey and why security matters"}]},{"at":12.9,"heading":"Core idea: packets","items":[{"at":13.05,"kind":"term","text":"Internet","detail":"a network of networks"},{"at":13.05,"kind":"text","text":"Data is split into small packets"},{"at":18.19,"kind":"text","text":"Each packet carries destination address"},{"at":23.12,"kind":"text","text":"Routers pass packets network to network"},{"at":27.71,"kind":"term","text":"IP address","detail":"postal address for a device on a network"}]},{"at":33.04,"heading":"Names, addresses, ports","items":[{"at":33.19,"kind":"term","text":"DNS","detail":"phone book: name → IP address"},{"at":40.78,"kind":"term","text":"Port","detail":"numbered door for one kind of service"},{"at":44.77,"kind":"text","text":"HTTP port 80; HTTPS port 443"}]},{"at":51.29,"heading":"HTTP vs HTTPS","items":[{"at":51.44,"kind":"text","text":"HTTP sends data as plain text"},{"at":56.05,"kind":"term","text":"HTTPS","detail":"TLS encryption + certificate; padlock shown"}]},{"at":68.62,"heading":"Defence basics","items":[{"at":68.77,"kind":"text","text":"Attackers scan ports, read HTTP; no permission = illegal"},{"at":74.89,"kind":"term","text":"Firewall","detail":"filter deciding what traffic may enter or leave"},{"at":78.96,"kind":"text","text":"Close unused ports; filter traffic; use HTTPS"}]},{"at":87.95,"heading":"Worked example: request","items":[{"at":88.1,"kind":"text","text":"Type website address, press Enter"},{"at":92.68,"kind":"text","text":"DNS: name → IP address"},{"at":96.88,"kind":"text","text":"Connect to web server on HTTPS port"},{"at":100.95,"kind":"text","text":"Browser sends encrypted HTTPS request"}]},{"at":104.78,"heading":"Worked example: response","items":[{"at":104.93,"kind":"text","text":"Server returns page encrypted; browser shows it"},{"at":109.14,"kind":"text","text":"HTTP-only site: all data travels as plain text"}]},{"at":114.55,"heading":"Quick check","items":[{"at":114.55,"kind":"question","text":"Which port does HTTPS use, and what does the padlock actually promise?"}]},{"at":123.71,"heading":"Answer","items":[{"at":123.86,"kind":"text","text":"HTTPS uses port 443"},{"at":123.86,"kind":"text","text":"Padlock = private encrypted connection, not honesty"}]},{"at":133.12,"heading":"Common mistakes","items":[{"at":133.27,"kind":"wrong","text":"Padlock = site is honest"},{"at":140.06,"kind":"wrong","text":"Leave unused ports open"},{"at":146.38,"kind":"wrong","text":"Use HTTP for private data"}]},{"at":154.79,"heading":"Recap and tip","items":[{"at":154.94,"kind":"text","text":"Packets move through networks via IP and DNS"},{"at":161.63,"kind":"text","text":"Ports = numbered doors; HTTPS uses 443"},{"at":168.07,"kind":"text","text":"Defenders close ports, filter with firewalls, use HTTPS"},{"at":174.7,"kind":"tip","text":"Before personal data: check HTTPS, then site trust"},{"at":181.96,"kind":"text","text":"Knowzone: hard ideas become simple"}]}],"check":{"at":123.36,"seconds":10,"question":"Which port does HTTPS use, and what does the padlock actually promise?","answer":"HTTPS uses port 443. The padlock promises a private encrypted connection, not that the site is honest."},"fps":30,"mouth":"","level":"","credits":[],"voice":"qwen3-tts-12hz-1.7b (cloned voice)"}